1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,585
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,585 --> 00:00:05,127
Episode 16.

3
00:00:05,127 --> 00:00:08,053
The Fox After Closing Time.

4
00:00:08,053 --> 00:00:11,079
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:11,079 --> 00:00:14,886
Why did the children compare several photographs?

6
00:00:14,886 --> 00:00:16,477
Ready?

7
00:00:16,477 --> 00:00:18,678
Let us find out.

8
00:00:18,678 --> 00:00:23,053
The school garden camera takes a picture after sunset.

9
00:00:23,053 --> 00:00:26,647
In it, two bright eyes shine near the fence.

10
00:00:26,647 --> 00:00:29,957
A tiny dragon, Leo suggests.

11
00:00:29,957 --> 00:00:33,920
A visitor with an excellent flashlight, says Pip.

12
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:36,889
Maya looks at the next picture.

13
00:00:36,889 --> 00:00:41,278
Now she can see pointed ears and a long, bushy tail.

14
00:00:41,278 --> 00:00:46,107
A local wildlife expert identifies the visitor as a fox.

15
00:00:46,107 --> 00:00:49,403
But we live in a city, Leo says.

16
00:00:49,403 --> 00:00:54,048
Cities can have wildlife, too, the expert explains.

17
00:00:54,048 --> 00:00:58,707
Gardens, parks, and quiet corners can provide shelter.

18
00:00:58,707 --> 00:01:02,599
Some foxes find ways to live close to people.

19
00:01:02,599 --> 00:01:06,363
The children study several nights of photographs.

20
00:01:06,363 --> 00:01:11,093
The fox does not appear at exactly the same time every evening.

21
00:01:11,093 --> 00:01:14,758
Sometimes the camera shows only a cat.

22
00:01:14,758 --> 00:01:18,380
Sometimes it shows leaves moving in the wind.

23
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:22,840
We should not call every pair of eyes a fox, Maya says.

24
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:27,883
They decide to observe from a distance and keep their food waste secured.

25
00:01:27,883 --> 00:01:31,505
They do not try to feed or touch the animal.

26
00:01:31,505 --> 00:01:37,002
Pip writes a polite invitation: Dear Fox, please attend our science club.

27
00:01:37,002 --> 00:01:39,460
The expert smiles.

28
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:43,238
The best invitation is space to remain wild.

29
00:01:43,238 --> 00:01:48,906
On the next photograph, the fox is walking away with its tail raised slightly.

30
00:01:48,906 --> 00:01:53,153
Leo writes the caption: The garden has a night shift.

31
00:01:53,153 --> 00:01:56,235
Pip adds: Attendance optional.

32
00:01:56,235 --> 00:01:58,721
No homework assigned.

33
00:01:58,721 --> 00:02:01,875
Now, a little more to discover.

34
00:02:01,875 --> 00:02:06,065
Some foxes live successfully in towns and cities.

35
00:02:06,065 --> 00:02:12,144
Their habitat can include gardens and green spaces as well as quieter places to shelter.

36
00:02:12,144 --> 00:02:16,122
Living near people does not make an animal a pet.

37
00:02:16,122 --> 00:02:21,761
Careful observation can help us learn about wildlife while leaving it undisturbed.

38
00:02:21,761 --> 00:02:24,928
Let us hear the main idea again.

39
00:02:24,928 --> 00:02:29,375
A nighttime camera revealed a fox near the school garden.

40
00:02:29,375 --> 00:02:33,977
The children learned that cities can contain wildlife habitats.

41
00:02:33,977 --> 00:02:40,014
They compared several pictures and chose to observe without approaching or feeding the visitor.

42
00:02:40,014 --> 00:02:43,153
Here are three useful expressions.

43
00:02:43,153 --> 00:02:46,335
You can listen, or say them with me.

44
00:02:46,335 --> 00:02:49,161
What usually happens at night?

45
00:02:49,161 --> 00:02:51,988
What usually happens at night?

46
00:02:51,988 --> 00:02:54,630
We saw it near the fence.

47
00:02:54,630 --> 00:02:57,272
We saw it near the fence.

48
00:02:57,272 --> 00:03:00,071
Let us give it some space.

49
00:03:00,071 --> 00:03:02,869
Let us give it some space.

50
00:03:02,869 --> 00:03:05,866
Remember our listening question?

51
00:03:05,866 --> 00:03:09,673
Why did the children compare several photographs?

52
00:03:09,673 --> 00:03:11,960
Here is the answer.

53
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:15,582
They wanted better evidence about the visitor.

54
00:03:15,582 --> 00:03:20,156
Not every picture or pair of shining eyes showed the same animal.

55
00:03:20,156 --> 00:03:24,119
And here is a question just for your imagination.

56
00:03:24,119 --> 00:03:28,806
What animals might share your neighborhood while most people are asleep?

57
00:03:28,806 --> 00:03:32,116
There is more than one possible answer.

58
00:03:32,116 --> 00:03:35,411
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
